1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
 We've made some good progress together over the last week and a half.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:07,840
 We now know what a state space model is, and we know how to construct one for general

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:12,160
 translating mechanical systems. And we've actually even built a few of these together

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:16,480
 when all we've been given is a sketch or a pictorial representation of the system.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:20,400
 On our journey to being well-rounded modeling engineers, we've now met three different

6
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:25,920
 representations of physical systems. These sketches of schematics, the pictorial representations,

7
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:30,400
 are the ODEs that we got from first principles modeling and our favorite kind,

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:35,520
 the state space models. Our next milestone, our next representation that we need to meet,

9
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:40,960
 is the block diagram. And over the course of this video, we're going to learn what a block diagram is,

10
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:44,880
 and then over the next few videos, we're going to learn how to convert our existing models,

11
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:52,640
 our state space and ODEs, into block diagrams. Block diagrams are visual maps, visual representations

12
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:58,800
 show us the flow of information in our model. And they're super, super useful. Number one,

13
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,920
 they give us a way of converting between other representations that might ordinarily be difficult.

14
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:09,120
 Number two, they reveal potential issues about our model or our controls, for example,

15
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:14,320
 causality. And number three, most importantly, I think, they make it really easy to see the

16
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:19,440
 flow of information and understand how our model is constructed. Block diagrams consist of two

17
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:25,280
 fundamental components, lines or arrows that we call signals, and blocks which operate on these

18
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:32,560
 signals. This block diagram shows the signal X of T entering a block called F, which operates on

19
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:39,040
 that signal to provide another signal called Y, which is F of X of T. In general, we're going

20
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:44,560
 to draw blocks as squares with the particular operator that they are written inside of the square.

21
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:50,560
 But for ease of reading and to line up with Simulink, the MATLAB based program for simulating

22
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:57,120
 block diagrams, some blocks we give special shapes. The sum, the gain, and the integrator blocks

23
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:02,720
 are examples of these. You should note that every fundamental operation generally has its own block,

24
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:08,320
 additions, traction, modification, division, calculus as well, integrators, derivatives.

25
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:13,200
 But that's what to say that you can't build your own blocks. And you can make a block,

26
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:19,200
 as complicated as you like, a block which takes in multiple inputs, does some operation on all

27
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,640
 those inputs, and then provides multiple outputs. At the end of the day, the block diagram representation

28
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:29,680
 is not unique. And there are lots of different ways that you could build a particular block diagram

29
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:36,560
 using say all fundamental components or using fewer components, fewer blocks that have more

30
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:40,960
 complicated functions. It really just depends on what you're trying to communicate and who you're

31
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:46,400
 trying to communicate it to. So with the basics in hand, over the next few videos, we're going

32
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:52,480
 to learn how to convert the models we already have seen, state space and ODEs, into block diagram

33
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:57,040
 representation. And we might even go back and look at some of the problems we've generated models

34
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:07,040
 and draw the block diagrams for those.
